1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,147
Hello, everyone.

2
00:00:01,147 --> 00:00:04,087
This is '전주 한옥마을' [jeon-ju ha-nong-ma-eul] (Jeonju Hanok Village),

3
00:00:04,087 --> 00:00:07,313
where graceful tiled roofs ripple like waves.

4
00:00:07,313 --> 00:00:09,034
Today, with me, Madam J,

5
00:00:09,034 --> 00:00:11,830
shall we learn the Korean of the heart?

6
00:00:17,130 --> 00:00:19,886
We feel so many emotions each day — joy, sadness,

7
00:00:19,886 --> 00:00:20,336
flutter.

8
00:00:20,336 --> 00:00:22,754
When you can put those feelings into words,

9
00:00:22,754 --> 00:00:24,160
Korean grows much warmer.

10
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:35,555
So today's theme is '감정 표현' [gam-jeong pyo-hyeon] (expressing emotions),

11
00:00:35,555 --> 00:00:38,803
and a step deeper — describing subtle feelings.

12
00:00:38,803 --> 00:00:43,640
We'll even catch emotions that look alike but differ ever so slightly.

13
00:00:47,810 --> 00:00:50,166
Strolling slowly through the hanok alleys,

14
00:00:50,166 --> 00:00:51,455
your heart settles too.

15
00:00:51,455 --> 00:00:53,923
Learning emotion words in such a calm place,

16
00:00:53,923 --> 00:00:55,830
they'll stay with you a long time.

17
00:00:58,750 --> 00:01:01,880
A little hint first: in Korean,

18
00:01:01,880 --> 00:01:08,846
most feeling words are '형용사' [hyeong-yong-sa] (adjectives) — words that paint a state,

19
00:01:08,846 --> 00:01:13,490
like '기쁘다' [gi-ppeu-da] (to be glad) and '슬프다' [seul-peu-da] (to be sad).

20
00:01:14,990 --> 00:01:15,223
Now,

21
00:01:15,223 --> 00:01:17,908
let's begin today's lesson in this cozy alley.

22
00:01:17,908 --> 00:01:20,650
Are you ready to gently look inside your heart?

23
00:01:24,940 --> 00:01:32,302
There are four core feelings to master today: '기쁘다' [gi-ppeu-da] (to be happy),

24
00:01:32,302 --> 00:01:37,210
'슬프다' [seul-peu-da] (to be sad), '긴장되다' [gin-jang-doe-da] (to feel nervous),

25
00:01:37,210 --> 00:01:39,998
and '속상하다' [sok-ssang-ha-da] (to be upset).

26
00:01:39,998 --> 00:01:43,010
Let's meet them one by one.

27
00:01:43,460 --> 00:01:43,888
First,

28
00:01:43,888 --> 00:01:50,158
'기쁘다' [gi-ppeu-da] (to be happy) — the feeling of your heart brightening when something good happens.

29
00:01:50,158 --> 00:01:52,510
A smile spreads across your face.

30
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:59,322
Use it like this: '합격 소식을 들어서 정말 기뻐요' (I'm so glad to hear I passed).

31
00:01:59,322 --> 00:02:03,300
Say it brightly — the listener grows glad with you.

32
00:02:05,410 --> 00:02:05,950
Second,

33
00:02:05,950 --> 00:02:12,510
'슬프다' [seul-peu-da] (to be sad) — that heavy feeling when your heart sinks and your eyes grow warm.

34
00:02:15,270 --> 00:02:18,528
'헤어지게 되어서 슬퍼요' [he-eo-ji-ge doe-eo-seo seul-peo-yo] (I'm sad because we have to part).

35
00:02:18,528 --> 00:02:19,592
When you're sad,

36
00:02:19,592 --> 00:02:23,050
saying it honestly like this can be a great comfort.

37
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:27,735
Third, '긴장되다' [gin-jang-doe-da] (to feel nervous) — heart pounding,

38
00:02:27,735 --> 00:02:30,609
body stiffening before something important.

39
00:02:30,609 --> 00:02:33,750
Picture the moment right before a presentation.

40
00:02:35,970 --> 00:02:40,650
'면접을 앞두고 있어서 너무 긴장돼요' (I'm so nervous with the interview ahead).

41
00:02:40,650 --> 00:02:44,379
Take a deep breath and soothe yourself: 'It's okay,

42
00:02:44,379 --> 00:02:45,330
I can do it'.

43
00:02:47,840 --> 00:02:48,348
Fourth,

44
00:02:48,348 --> 00:02:53,716
'속상하다' [sok-ssang-ha-da] (to be upset) — when things don't go your way and your heart aches,

45
00:02:53,716 --> 00:02:56,980
a sore, stuffy feeling. It's used very often.

46
00:03:00,790 --> 00:03:04,864
'실수를 해서 많이 속상해요' (I'm really upset because I made a mistake).

47
00:03:04,864 --> 00:03:08,804
Don't bottle it up — share it like this with someone close,

48
00:03:08,804 --> 00:03:10,340
and it'll feel lighter.

49
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:19,240
One more: '설레다' [seol-le-da] (to feel a flutter) — the pleasant thrill of your heart pattering as you look forward to something good.

50
00:03:21,660 --> 00:03:27,707
There are more: '화나다' [hwa-na-da] (to be angry) and '걱정되다' [geok-jjeong-doe-da] (to be worried).

51
00:03:27,707 --> 00:03:30,970
Pick the one that fits the moment.

52
00:03:36,730 --> 00:03:39,191
Let's recap: glad, sad, nervous, upset.

53
00:03:39,191 --> 00:03:42,850
Just these four already let you share a whole day's heart.

54
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:53,123
Now a step further: how to say 'why' you feel that way,

55
00:03:53,123 --> 00:03:54,781
joining the cause to the feeling.

56
00:03:54,781 --> 00:03:56,790
Let's learn the link that connects them.

57
00:04:03,480 --> 00:04:11,097
That link is '-아서' [--a-seo] and '-어서' [--eo-seo] — connective endings that join a cause.

58
00:04:11,097 --> 00:04:16,250
Bright vowels take '-아서' [--a-seo], the rest take '-어서' [--eo-seo].

59
00:04:24,190 --> 00:04:32,516
An example: '시험이 끝나서 기뻐요' [si-heo-mi kkeun-na-seo gi-ppeo-yo] (I'm glad because the exam is over).

60
00:04:32,516 --> 00:04:36,007
Cause first, feeling next.

61
00:04:36,007 --> 00:04:40,170
'끝나다' plus '-아서' [--a-seo] becomes '끝나서'.

62
00:04:42,350 --> 00:04:45,029
Hear it? The 'why' flows right into the feeling.

63
00:04:45,029 --> 00:04:48,880
Speaking this way lets others understand your heart much more deeply.

64
00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:59,559
Now the sad side: '비가 와서 속상해요' [bi-ga wa-seo sok-ssang-hae-yo] (I'm upset because it rained).

65
00:04:59,559 --> 00:05:01,727
'오다' [o-da] turns into '와서' [wa-seo].

66
00:05:01,727 --> 00:05:06,890
You can picture a child whose picnic was canceled.

67
00:05:08,060 --> 00:05:10,478
Every feeling has a reason.

68
00:05:10,478 --> 00:05:13,254
Reveal it gently with '-아서/어서' [a-seo-eo-seo] [--a-seo-/-eo-seo],

69
00:05:13,254 --> 00:05:18,000
and it becomes not a complaint but a heartfelt story.

70
00:05:22,070 --> 00:05:27,631
Nervousness too: '발표가 있어서 긴장돼요' [bal-pyo-ga i-sseo-seo gin-jang-dwae-yo] (I'm nervous because I have a presentation).

71
00:05:27,631 --> 00:05:31,363
Naming the cause makes it easy for others to cheer,

72
00:05:31,363 --> 00:05:32,680
'You'll do great'.

73
00:05:35,700 --> 00:05:42,702
Add flutter: '오랜 친구를 만나서 설레요' (I feel a flutter because I'm meeting an old friend).

74
00:05:42,702 --> 00:05:46,330
The anticipation rides on '-아서' [--a-seo], pit-a-pat.

75
00:05:47,050 --> 00:05:52,698
One more: '칭찬을 들어서 뿌듯해요' [ching-cha-neul deu-reo-seo ppu-deu-tae-yo] (I feel proud because I was praised).

76
00:05:52,698 --> 00:05:56,342
'뿌듯하다' [ppu-deu-ta-da] (proud and content) is that warm,

77
00:05:56,342 --> 00:05:57,800
filling feeling.

78
00:05:58,590 --> 00:06:02,094
In sum: 'cause + 아서/어서 + feeling'.

79
00:06:02,094 --> 00:06:07,143
This one line conveys both what you feel and why,

80
00:06:07,143 --> 00:06:08,380
all at once.

81
00:06:18,340 --> 00:06:22,238
Now today's highlight: feelings that look similar but differ in texture.

82
00:06:22,238 --> 00:06:26,190
Knowing these subtle shades lets you paint your heart with real delicacy.

83
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:40,492
'속상하다' [sok-ssang-ha-da] is when things go wrong; '서운하다' [seo-un-ha-da] (to feel let down) is the quiet hurt when someone doesn't meet what you'd hoped,

84
00:06:40,492 --> 00:06:45,600
like '연락이 없어서 서운했어요' [yeol-la-gi eop-sseo-seo seo-un-hae-sseo-yo] (I felt let down that you didn't call).

85
00:06:46,050 --> 00:06:51,503
'뿌듯하다' [ppu-deu-ta-da] (proud and content) is the warm fullness after real effort.

86
00:06:51,503 --> 00:06:55,717
Say '해내서 뿌듯해요' [hae-nae-seo ppu-deu-tae-yo] (I feel proud that I pulled it off),

87
00:06:55,717 --> 00:06:58,030
and the reward carries over.

88
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:04,716
Two feelings at an ending: lingering regret is '아쉽다' [a-swip-tta] (what a pity),

89
00:07:04,716 --> 00:07:10,517
and the relief of setting a burden down is '홀가분하다' [hol-ga-bun-ha-da] (light and free).

90
00:07:10,517 --> 00:07:12,820
One farewell can hold both.

91
00:07:13,270 --> 00:07:16,953
For deep emotion: '벅차다' [beok-cha-da] (overwhelmed,

92
00:07:16,953 --> 00:07:21,034
heart brimming) and '뭉클하다' [mung-keul-ha-da] (deeply moved,

93
00:07:21,034 --> 00:07:22,925
throat tightening),

94
00:07:22,925 --> 00:07:28,200
as in '그 말에 마음이 뭉클했어요' (those words deeply moved me).

95
00:07:30,980 --> 00:07:36,253
Anticipation has two shades: the gentle swell of '설레다' [seol-le-da],

96
00:07:36,253 --> 00:07:40,664
and the fast beat of '두근거리다' [du-geun-geo-ri-da] (heart pounding),

97
00:07:40,664 --> 00:07:46,800
as in '첫 여행이라 두근거려요' [cheot yeo-haeng-i-ra du-geun-geo-ryeo-yo] (my heart's pounding — it's my first trip).

98
00:07:47,250 --> 00:07:52,992
Now expressions painted with '마음' [ma-eum] (the heart): when worry lifts,

99
00:07:52,992 --> 00:07:55,594
'마음이 놓이다' [ma-eu-mi no-i-da] (to feel relieved),

100
00:07:55,594 --> 00:08:00,260
as in '무사하다니 마음이 놓여요' [mu-sa-ha-da-ni ma-eu-mi no-yeo-yo] (I'm so relieved you're safe).

101
00:08:02,940 --> 00:08:04,482
Weighed by worry,

102
00:08:04,482 --> 00:08:07,929
'마음이 무겁다' [ma-eu-mi mu-geop-tta] (heavy-hearted); shaken off,

103
00:08:07,929 --> 00:08:10,287
'마음이 가볍다' [ma-eu-mi ga-byeop-tta] (light-hearted).

104
00:08:10,287 --> 00:08:14,460
Painting the heart as weight — so very Korean.

105
00:08:17,840 --> 00:08:21,410
When emotion surges and your throat catches,

106
00:08:21,410 --> 00:08:23,033
'울컥하다' [ul-keo-ka-da] (to well up).

107
00:08:23,033 --> 00:08:30,580
When gratitude or sorrow floods in at once: '편지를 읽다가 울컥했어요' [pyeon-ji-reul ik-tta-ga ul-keo-kae-sseo-yo] (I welled up reading the letter).

108
00:08:31,030 --> 00:08:32,462
See? Upset and let-down,

109
00:08:32,462 --> 00:08:35,625
brimming and moved — each a slightly different grain.

110
00:08:35,625 --> 00:08:37,833
The more you catch these fine shades,

111
00:08:37,833 --> 00:08:40,340
the deeper and warmer your Korean becomes.

112
00:08:46,620 --> 00:08:50,779
Feelings aren't meant to be swallowed alone but shared.

113
00:08:50,779 --> 00:08:55,090
Feeling another's heart together — that's '공감' [gong-gam] (empathy).

114
00:08:58,540 --> 00:09:03,762
The classic empathy line: '많이 속상했겠어요' [ma-ni sok-ssang-haet-kke-sseo-yo] (You must have been so upset).

115
00:09:03,762 --> 00:09:08,215
'-겠어요' [--get-eo-yo] is a tender ending that guesses at another's heart.

116
00:09:08,215 --> 00:09:11,210
This one phrase melts the tension away.

117
00:09:14,180 --> 00:09:18,057
For happy news, rejoice big together: '얼마나 기뻤어요' [eol-ma-na gi-ppeo-sseo-yo]!

118
00:09:18,057 --> 00:09:20,351
(How glad you must have been!

119
00:09:20,351 --> 00:09:24,070
), '정말 설렜겠어요' [jeong-mal seol-let-kke-sseo-yo] (You must have been so thrilled).

120
00:09:24,070 --> 00:09:26,760
Echoing the heart doubles the joy.

121
00:09:27,210 --> 00:09:31,884
Sometimes being there comforts more than perfect words: '괜찮아요,

122
00:09:31,884 --> 00:09:35,352
제가 곁에 있을게요' (It's okay, I'll be by your side).

123
00:09:35,352 --> 00:09:37,840
Remember the warmth of this line.

124
00:09:38,290 --> 00:09:40,082
When such hearts pile up,

125
00:09:40,082 --> 00:09:43,163
they become Korea's '정' [jeong] (jeong) — the deep,

126
00:09:43,163 --> 00:09:46,317
warm bond between people who've shared time,

127
00:09:46,317 --> 00:09:48,180
beyond what words can say.

128
00:09:51,050 --> 00:09:55,025
'정' [jeong] shows in little things — asking '밥은 먹었어요' [ba-beun meo-geo-sseo-yo]?

129
00:09:55,025 --> 00:09:56,407
(Have you eaten?

130
00:09:56,407 --> 00:09:59,604
), quietly adding one more side dish.

131
00:09:59,604 --> 00:10:02,110
That is the jeong of Koreans.

132
00:10:05,950 --> 00:10:11,930
Today we learned to paint the heart in Korean — four core feelings,

133
00:10:11,930 --> 00:10:15,946
joining causes with '-아서/어서' [a-seo-eo-seo] [--a-seo-/-eo-seo], subtle nuances,

134
00:10:15,946 --> 00:10:18,980
empathy and comfort, and even '정' [jeong].

135
00:10:25,290 --> 00:10:27,002
What did your heart feel today?

136
00:10:27,002 --> 00:10:28,770
Share it gently in the comments.

137
00:10:28,770 --> 00:10:33,520
Let's meet again for the Korean of the heart — and don't forget to subscribe and like!

